Let a tablet hide Chromium's browser UI without pretending to be a phone.

"Who draws the browser" and "what kind of screen is this" were the same
question here: IsAuraShellMobilePhoneUi() decided both, so a tablet could have
Chromium's tab strip and toolbar with a tablet's user agent, or a phone's
everything, and nothing in between. The shell's wide-screen UI needs the third
combination -- its own tab strip and toolbar, with a tablet's user agent,
scrollbars and pointer behaviour.

The drawing half moves to IsAuraShellChromeHiddenByShell(), set from the
browserChrome startup option and fixed for the run: unfolding a foldable or
docking a 2-in-1 changes ui_family, and must not put Chromium's tab strip back
on screen mid-session. Unset, it still means what it meant before -- the shell
draws on a phone and Chromium draws everywhere else -- so an app that passes
nothing sees no change.

What stops being drawn when it is on, all of it already conditioned on the old
predicate: the tab strip (ShouldDrawTabStrip), the toolbar (IsToolbarVisible),
the location bar (IsLocationBarVisible), the toolbar's children and the
bookmark bar (BrowserView::OnOhosUiFamilyChanged and
ToolbarView::OnOhosUiFamilyChanged, which also remember what they hid so
switching back restores it). Elsewhere and already converted: the context menu
(shell_context_menu_ohos.cc), the download UI (shell_downloads_prefs_ohos.cc)
and permission prompts (shell_permission_prompt_ohos.cc).

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

